---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const stats = [
  { n: '110', l: 'Requirements' },
  { n: '14', l: 'Families' },
  { n: '320', l: 'Objectives' },
  { n: '1,731', l: 'Tagged claims' },
  { n: '14', l: 'Marked low confidence' },
];
---

<Base
  title="CMMC Dictionary"
  description="What assessors accepted, rejected, and never asked for, recorded for each of the 110 NIST SP 800-171 Rev 2 requirements and tagged to the assessment objective it bears on."
>
  <Fragment slot="mast">
    <p class="eyebrow">
      <b>NIST SP 800-171 Rev 2</b> · <span>CMMC Level 2</span> · <span>110 requirements · 320 objectives</span>
    </p>
    <h1>Where the bar actually is</h1>
    <p class="thesis">
      Every other reference tells you what the 110 requirements <em>say</em>. You can already read that.
      This one records what assessors <em>accepted</em>, what they <em>rejected</em>, and what was money
      nobody needed to spend, tagged to the individual assessment objective it bears on.
    </p>
    <ul class="stats" aria-label="Corpus size">
      {stats.map(({ n, l }) => (
        <li class="stat">
          <span class="n">{n}</span>
          <span class="l">{l}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="how-to-read">
    <h2 id="how-to-read">How a requirement page reads</h2>
    <p>
      Each of the 110 requirements has its own page, written as <span class="id">3.1.1</span> and so on.
      The page quotes the requirement, lists its assessment objectives, and then records five things.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Sufficient</dt>
      <dd>Implementations an assessor has accepted as meeting the requirement. This is the bar.</dd>
      <dt>Insufficient</dt>
      <dd>Implementations an assessor has rejected, and the reason given. Most of these looked fine on paper.</dd>
      <dt>Over-engineering</dt>
      <dd>Work that was done and never asked for. Spending here does not raise the score.</dd>
      <dt>Edge cases</dt>
      <dd>Situations where the answer is still open, or where two assessors have read the same objective differently. Both readings are kept.</dd>
      <dt>Evidence examples</dt>
      <dd>Artifacts that have passed: the screenshot, export, or record an assessor accepted as proof.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Every claim in those sections carries the letters of the objectives it bears on, so a claim about <span class="id">3.1.1</span> is tagged <span class="id">[a]</span>, <span class="id">[c]</span>, or whichever apply.
      That tagging is what makes a page per objective possible.
      The question "what satisfies <span class="id">3.1.1[c]</span>" has its own answer, separate from the requirement as a whole.
    </p>

    <h2 id="low-confidence">Why 14 requirements are marked low confidence</h2>
    <p>
      For 14 of the 110 requirements the source material was thin. Their pages still exist, but most of
      the five sections say so plainly instead of restating the Assessment Guide to fill the space.
      Each of those requirements is labeled <strong>low confidence</strong> on its page and in the grid.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hiding them would make the corpus look more complete than it is. Publishing the gap tells you which
      pages to trust less and which ones need a correction from someone who has sat through the assessment.
    </p>
  </section>

